{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_qwdxn6ffxaqya27apjnqx72vlolmhe5gaicygdx56nce5qahtoaa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**Verification complete — all four phases pass. No changes to the Rust translation were needed; it was already byte-exact.**\n\n## Phase A artifacts (`translation/`)\n| file | content |\n|---|---|\n| `SYMBOLS.md` | 3 C exports (`tflac_pack_u64le`, `tflac_md5_addsample`, `update_md5`); Rust `.so` exports all 3, `comm -23` diff **empty**; 0 unresolved non-libc symbols; verified struct layouts (`tflac_md5`=88, `tflac`=96) against the C compiler |\n| `ERRORS.md` | 18 rows. Mechanical grep proved the library has **no** error codes, sentinels, asserts, enums or range checks — its only branch is `if (m->pos >= 64)`. Rows therefore cover unsigned-wraparound/modulo behaviour, `bits` truncation, out-of-range `pos`, overflow, sign-extension, the 136-sample minimum, and NULL |\n| `CONFIGS.md` | 12 axes → 28 combination rows, incl. the low-level entry points driven directly and 4 composed-pipeline sequence rows |\n\n## Phases B–D\n- `tests/common/mod.rs` — loads **both** `.so`s via `libloading`, compares the full 88/96-byte context image (padding included) + return value; fixed-seed xorshift64* PRNG.\n- `tests/phase_b_valid.rs` — 30 tests, one per `CONFIGS.md` row. All pass.\n- `tests/phase_c_errors.rs` — 20 tests, one per `ERRORS.md` row + generic boundary grids. All pass, incl. a `fork()`-based NULL test asserting **identical SIGSEGV** from both libraries.\n- `run_tests.sh` — builds both libs, diffs `nm -D` (0 missing), then runs the matrix: default / `--no-default-features` / `--all-features` (crate declares no features), plus C `-O0`/`-O2` × Rust debug/release. **50/50 pass in every configuration.**\n- No binary/driver exists in either build (`CMakeLists.txt` has no `add_executable`, `Cargo.toml` has no `[[bin]]`), so the stdout-comparison gate is N/A.\n\n## One notable finding (harness bug, not a translation bug)\nEight tests initially failed. Root cause: the C fold-down loop\n```c\nbytes = m->pos % 64;  while (bytes--) m->buffer[bytes] = m->buffer[64 + bytes];\n```\nreads up to `buffer[126]` — **55 bytes past the 72-byte array** — whenever a caller supplies `pos % 64 > 8`. The Rust does the identical pointer arithmetic; the two impls merely read different adjacent stack bytes because each had its own allocation. Fixed by embedding every context in a 192-byte deterministically pre-filled guard region, which both makes the OOB read reproducible and proves neither side ever *writes* out of bounds (`guard_intact()` asserted after every call). Documented in `ERRORS.md`.\n\nOnly edits outside `translation/`: `c_src/build/` (created by the prescribed CMake command); `c_src` sources untouched."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":514,"cache_read_input_tokens":118201,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":514,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ec19a435-f23a-4029-b4cd-8c02a3e83a46","uuid":"921765ab-9433-46c3-ac78-e4c1390a665f","timestamp":"2026-09-05T02:42:01.524Z"}
